  "bloques": [
    {
      "tipo": "explicacion",
      "titulo": {
        "en": "Behind the wheel"
      },
      "texto": {
        "en": "Useful driving verbs: **conducir**, **adelantar**, **frenar**, **girar** and **aparcar**. **Abrocharse el cinturón** means to fasten a seat belt; **llevar el carné de conducir** means to carry a driving licence.\n\nRoad features include **carriles**, **semáforos** and **pasos de cebra** for **peatones**. The general maximum speed for cars and motorcycles on Spanish motorways is 120 km/h; obey any lower speed shown on signs.\n\nThere may be **atascos**; drivers can get **multas** for speeding or parking badly; tyres can get punctures; cars can run out of petrol. From 2026, vehicles registered in Spain must use a connected **baliza V-16** to signal a vehicle immobilized by breakdown or accident. The beacon does not call the emergency services or a tow truck, so contact them separately.\n\nCommon road rules use impersonal forms: **no se puede adelantar** (overtaking is not allowed); **está prohibido aparcar** (parking is prohibited); **hay que ceder el paso a los peatones en los pasos de cebra** (drivers must give way to pedestrians); **es obligatorio abrocharse el cinturón** (seat belts must be fastened)."
      }
    },
